From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sid Manning <atx0x200@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 12 Jul 2026 16:20:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] xcommon: avoid non-literal format string passed to vfprintf

nfs_error() built its actual format string at runtime by
concatenating the caller's fmt with "\n", then passed that computed
string to vfprintf(). nfs-utils enables -Werror=format=2
unconditionally, and Clang's -Wformat-nonliteral (part of
-Wformat=2) flags any vfprintf()-family call whose format argument
is not provably a string literal, which errors out the build.

Passing 'fmt' straight through to vfprintf() and printing the
trailing newline separately keeps nfs_error()'s existing behavior
(message followed by a newline) while matching the format-attribute
forwarding idiom both GCC and Clang recognize as safe for this kind
of printf wrapper, since nfs_error() itself is already declared
with __attribute__((format(printf, 1, 2))) in xcommon.h.

Signed-off-by: Sid Manning <atx0x200@gmail.com>
---
 support/nfs/xcommon.c | 6 ++----
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/support/nfs/xcommon.c b/support/nfs/xcommon.c
index 3eda7d343..0bcd9552d 100644
--- a/support/nfs/xcommon.c
+++ b/support/nfs/xcommon.c
@@ -94,13 +94,11 @@
 void
 nfs_error (const char *fmt, ...) {
      va_list args;
-     char *fmt2;
 
-     fmt2 = xstrconcat2 (fmt, "\n");
      va_start (args, fmt);
-     vfprintf (stderr, fmt2, args);
+     vfprintf (stderr, fmt, args);
      va_end (args);
-     free (fmt2);
+     fputc ('\n', stderr);
 }
 
 /* Make a canonical pathname from PATH.  Returns a freshly malloced string.
--
2.46.2
